virglrenderer: H.264 encoding on the Mac's media engine (VideoToolbox)

What: the VideoToolbox video backend (virgl-videotoolbox-decode.patch) also
encodes. The host offers H.264 Constrained Baseline, Main and High with the
ENCODE entrypoint (up to 4096x2304) when VideoToolbox has a hardware H.264
encoder; OMACVM_VIDEO_NO_ENCODE=1 leaves encoding out (the guest encodes in
software). Per frame: begin_frame takes a picture from the compression
session's pool and vrend copies the guest's NV12 plane textures into it (GPU
blit into the IOSurface planes, CPU readback as fallback); encode_bitstream
applies the guest's bitrate, frame rate and GOP (clamped) and forces a key frame
for IDR pictures; end_frame waits for the frame and hands vrend an Annex B access
unit (SPS and PPS before every key frame). Real-time, low-latency rate control,
no frame reordering (no B frames).
vrend_video.c: the coded-data and feedback resources stay referenced from
encode_bitstream to the end of the frame (a guest could unref them in between),
and a coded buffer that cannot be mapped is not written to (it was a NULL
memcpy).
Why: WebRTC (camera, screen sharing) and ffmpeg h264_vaapi in the VM encode on
the CPU otherwise.
Upstream: OmacVM addition to its own backend; the vrend_video.c fixes are
upstreamable.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-video-encode.c (30 frames 320x240 through the virgl
video protocol: feedback, SPS/PPS/IDR, decode back with VideoToolbox, luma
PSNR > 30 dB; measured 54.9 dB at 2 Mbit/s).
